From: phillip.choi <phillip.choi@hardkernel.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ODROID-C5: Fix Playback Hangs on Ubuntu 26.04

On playback restart, Poll() blocked after CAPTURE & OUTPUT buffers are fully
filled. Because Poll() couldn't receive any events from the V4L2 device after
those buffers are filled, no data can be dispatched from the device. Poll(-1)
waits forever and blocks the decode thread -> playback hangs. Use a finite
timeout so the decode thread can wake up and flush/dequeue those buffers.

Adapted to chromium 149 (std::array pollfds / pollfds.data()).

Signed-off-by: phillip.choi <phillip.choi@hardkernel.com>
---
diff --git a/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_device.cc b/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_device.cc
--- a/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_device.cc
+++ b/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_device.cc
@@ -351,6 +351,7 @@
   std::array<struct pollfd, 2> pollfds;
   nfds_t nfds;
   int pollfd = -1;
+  int ret;
 
   pollfds[0].fd = device_poll_interrupt_fd_.get();
   pollfds[0].events = POLLIN | POLLERR;
@@ -364,10 +365,18 @@
     nfds++;
   }
 
-  if (HANDLE_EINTR(poll(pollfds.data(), nfds, -1)) == -1) {
+  // ODROID-C5: the Amlogic stateful decoder can leave poll() with no pending
+  // buffer-done or event during a playback restart/flush (all CAPTURE+OUTPUT
+  // buffers full), so an infinite poll() wedges the decode thread and playback
+  // hangs. Use a finite timeout so the thread wakes and flushes/dequeues.
+  ret = HANDLE_EINTR(poll(pollfds.data(), nfds, 500));
+  if (ret == -1) {
     VPLOGF(1) << "poll() failed";
     return false;
   }
+  if (ret == 0) {
+    DVLOGF(3) << "poll() timeout";
+  }
   *event_pending = (pollfd != -1 && pollfds[pollfd].revents & POLLPRI);
   return true;
 }
